# Dashboard composition and card features

Arrange a dashboard around the few questions a household asks, then choose the cards. A card holds content, a view sets the layout, and a feature is a control inside a supported card. They are not interchangeable.

These examples are simulated with invented records. They do not call Home Assistant.

<h2 id="layouts">Four native view layouts</h2>

| Native view | Rule | Limit |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Sections | Group related tasks under descriptive headings. Put urgent exceptions before routine summaries. | Keep the native section order, grid sizing and the editor’s placement rules. |
| Masonry | Let each card keep its own height and make each one understandable alone. | Do not promise a left-to-right reading order from visual columns. Check the real keyboard and reading order. |
| Panel | Give one task the full width, such as a map or floor plan. | Give a text alternative, and keep controls outside precision overlays. |
| Sidebar | Put the main task first and related context second. | The related content must stay useful when the layout collapses on a narrow screen. |

Grid, horizontal stack and vertical stack are card containers, not extra view types. Avoid nested stacks that shrink labels or make unrelated cards look like one control. Prefer responsive sections when a row of controls does not fit. Native view visibility hides navigation. It is not an authorisation boundary, so keep account permissions separate.

<h2 id="cards">Card hierarchy and generated dashboards</h2>

Use Heading for a task title, Tile for a concise entity with suitable features, Area for a real configured area and Shortcut for an identified destination or action. A Button card needs an action name. A device name alone does not say whether pressing it opens details or changes state. Home summary, toggle-group and generated overview cards keep their native membership and eligibility rules. Counts must include unavailable members or explain why they are left out.

A Conditional card controls whether its child shows. Entity filter selects matching entities. Neither shows that a hidden entity is safe, off, absent or forbidden. Tell an intentionally empty result from failed data, and keep another route to essential controls. The native starting, error and empty-state cards keep their real error context. Do not make a failed card look like an empty household.

